C = [
("h1", "6. One Died for All"),
("p", "Claim: in 2 Corinthians 5:14–21 Paul reasons from a single death to a single conclusion, and the conclusion is about the living. The passage says that one died for all, that therefore all were dead, and that the living should live unto him who died for them. It then says that God reconciled us to himself, that he made him to be sin for us, and that we might be made the righteousness of God in him. The passage is dense with different words for the Cross. The volume reads them in order."),
("sub", "6.1 The reasoning of verses 14 and 15"),
("p", "“For the love of Christ constraineth us; because we thus judge, that if one died for all, then were all dead: And that he died for all, that they which live should not henceforth live unto themselves, but unto him which died for them, and rose again” (2 Corinthians 5:14, 15). The structure is an inference. Paul and his companions judge that if one died for all, then all were dead. The purpose of the death is stated: that they which live should not henceforth live unto themselves, but unto him which died for them and rose again."),
("ctbl", "What 2 Corinthians 5:14–15 says and what it leaves unsaid.", [
    ("The clause", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“the love of Christ constraineth us”", "Christ’s love presses Paul and his companions", "Whether the love is Christ’s love for them or theirs for him; the clause reads either way"),
    ("“if one died for all, then were all dead”", "One died for all, so all were dead", "Who “all” includes; whether the second “all” is the same persons as the first"),
    ("“that they which live should not henceforth live unto themselves”", "The purpose is that the living no longer live for themselves", "Whether “they which live” are all persons or a part"),
    ("“but unto him which died for them, and rose again”", "The living live for him who died and rose", "Nothing about how the death works"),
  ], [0.30, 0.36, 0.34]),
("p", "Thomas Constable reads the second “all” as believers: “all believers died in the Person of their representative, Jesus Christ.” The volume agrees that the second clause is about those who live with the life of Christ, and that the passage’s result is about them: “they which live.” It does not add, as Constable does, that the “all” of the first clause is believers only, because the clause does not say so. The first clause says “one died for all.” The volume reports the words and notices that the sentence moves from “all” to “they which live.” Chapter 15 says what the sentence leaves unsaid."),
("sub", "6.2 “A new creature” (verses 16 and 17)"),
("p", "“Wherefore henceforth know we no man after the flesh: yea, though we have known Christ after the flesh, yet now henceforth know we him no more. Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new” (2 Corinthians 5:16, 17). The passage draws from the death of Christ a change in how Paul knows persons, and it states the result for any man who is in Christ. The new creature is in Christ. The condition is stated: “if any man be in Christ.” The study on new creation carries the full account of the phrase. This volume notes only that the new creature is a result stated for those who are in Christ, and that it sits between the death in verse 14 and the reconciliation in verse 18."),
("sub", "6.3 Reconciliation and the word of reconciliation (verses 18 to 20)"),
("p", "“And all things are of God, who hath reconciled us to himself by Jesus Christ, and hath given to us the ministry of reconciliation; To wit, that God was in Christ, reconciling the world unto himself, not imputing their trespasses unto them; and hath committed unto us the word of reconciliation” (2 Corinthians 5:18, 19). Two things are said that need care. First, God reconciled “us” to himself by Jesus Christ, and gave “us” the ministry. Second, God was in Christ “reconciling the world unto himself, not imputing their trespasses unto them.” The participle is present: God was reconciling, and the ministry is the word of reconciliation committed to “us.”"),
("p", "The passage does not say that the world was reconciled in the sense of being saved. It says that God was reconciling the world, that he was not imputing trespasses, and that he committed to Paul and his companions a word. Then: “Now then we are ambassadors for Christ, as though God did beseech you by us: we pray you in Christ’s stead, be ye reconciled to God” (2 Corinthians 5:20). The appeal is made to be reconciled. A reconciliation already finished for every person would not need the appeal. The volume reads the passage as written, with the world as the sphere of God’s reconciling and the appeal as the means by which individuals are called to it."),
("sub", "6.4 “Made him to be sin for us” (verse 21)"),
("p", "“For he hath made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin; that we might be made the righteousness of God in him” (2 Corinthians 5:21). The verse has a subject, God; an object, him; a thing he is made, sin; a beneficiary, us; and a purpose, that we might be made the righteousness of God in him. The verse does not say that Christ became a sinner. It says that he knew no sin and that God made him to be sin. It does not explain how. The word “sin” is not defined. It is one of the places in Paul where the sentence is the entire teaching, and a reader who adds an account has gone beyond it."),
("tbl", "2 Corinthians 5:14–21, the Cross in each verse.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("2Cor 5:14", "“if one died for all, then were all dead”", "One for all; all dead"),
    ("2Cor 5:15", "“unto him which died for them, and rose again”", "Death and resurrection; the living live to him"),
    ("2Cor 5:18", "“who hath reconciled us to himself by Jesus Christ”", "Reconciled us; by Jesus Christ"),
    ("2Cor 5:19", "“God was in Christ, reconciling the world unto himself, not imputing their trespasses unto them”", "The world; not imputing trespasses; present participle"),
    ("2Cor 5:20", "“be ye reconciled to God”", "The appeal to be reconciled"),
    ("2Cor 5:21", "“he hath made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin”", "Made sin for us; Christ knew no sin"),
    ("2Cor 5:21", "“that we might be made the righteousness of God in him”", "Purpose: righteousness of God, in him"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "6.5 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("p", "The Cross is here a death for all, from which Paul and his companions reason that all were dead and that the living should live to Christ. It is the ground of a new creation for any man in Christ, of God’s reconciling the world without imputing trespasses, of an appeal to be reconciled, and of a transaction in which Christ is made sin and “we” are made the righteousness of God in him. It is not given a single figure, and the figures are not reconciled by the passage. The passage is about the ministry that follows from the Cross as much as about the Cross."),
]
